"/raw" =
  @tree
  "/sys/lede": "File and media ingestion · original source"
  "/text": "Send Codex this as the media/file-ingestion spec:
            
            > **File + media ingestion: raw artifact, native representation, anchors, provenance, then crystallization**
            >
            > I want the ingestion model to stay extremely simple and Shrine-native. Do **not** build a giant importer framework or permanent host-side application layer.
            >
            > The core rule is:
            >
            > ```text
            > external artifact
            >     ↓
            > preserve raw source exactly
            >     ↓
            > expose a faithful native representation in NS
            >     ↓
            > optionally create semantic anchors into that representation
            >     ↓
            > retain provenance of changes/interpretations
            >     ↓
            > let useful repeated interpretation crystallize into Foil
            > ```
            >
            > The file format determines how the artifact can be addressed. It does **not** determine the ontology Shrine must create from it.
            >
            > ## 1. Always preserve the raw artifact
            >
            > Every ingested thing should retain the exact original:
            >
            > ```text
            > Artifact#N
            > ├── raw source / bytes
            > ├── hash / version / provenance
            > └── native representation
            > ```
            >
            > Do not throw the original away after parsing.
            >
            > Raw source is evidence and allows re-interpretation later.
            >
            > ## 2. Also create a raw native representation
            >
            > Even if there is **zero semantic interpretation**, the artifact should still become usable native material.
            >
            > Examples:
            >
            > ```text
            > image
            >   -> Image
            >      └── pixels : Matrix<Pixel>
            >
            > text
            >   -> Text / Sequence<Char|Span>
            >
            > markdown
            >   -> document/block tree where useful
            >
            > json
            >   -> tree/map/native values
            >
            > csv
            >   -> rows/cells/values or some equivalent native collection
            >
            > xlsx
            >   -> sheets/cells/formulas/ranges
            >
            > pdf
            >   -> pages/text/layout/images/tables where recoverable
            > ```
            >
            > This does **not** mean:
            >
            > ```text
            > CSV must become CsvDocument
            > JSON must become JsonDocument
            > ```
            >
            > If a CSV semantically ends up becoming:
            >
            > ```text
            > Many<Person>
            > ```
            >
            > or:
            >
            > ```text
            > Organization.people
            > ```
            >
            > or:
            >
            > ```text
            > List<Money>
            > ```
            >
            > that is fine.
            >
            > The serialization structure is evidence and addressing, not ontology.
            >
            > ## 3. Three ways of interpreting artifacts
            >
            > The user/model should be able to work from:
            >
            > ```text
            > INSPECT
            > SELECT
            > DESCRIBE
            > ```
            >
            > independently or together.
            >
            > Examples:
            >
            > ```text
            > Inspect this image
            > Select this table
            > ""this is Alice""
            > ""make these rows People""
            > ""extract these totals""
            > ""track this vehicle""
            > ```
            >
            > No description should be mandatory.
            >
            > An image with no description is still:
            >
            > ```text
            > Image#4
            > └── pixels : Matrix<Pixel>
            > ```
            >
            > A PDF with no description is still a native document representation.
            >
            > Semantic interpretation is optional.
            >
            > ## 4. Anchors are exact correspondence, especially for media
            >
            > Anchors relate a concrete part of an artifact to an exact native Thing/path.
            >
            > Conceptually:
            >
            > ```text
            > Anchor
            > ├── artifactRef
            > ├── selector
            > │   ├── path
            > │   ├── cell/range
            > │   ├── text span
            > │   ├── bbox
            > │   ├── polygon
            > │   ├── mask
            > │   ├── timestamp range
            > │   └── object track
            > ├── nativeRef / nativePath
            > ├── provenance
            > └── confidence / interpretation metadata where useful
            > ```
            >
            > Examples:
            >
            > ```text
            > employees.csv row 18
            >     ↔ Person#17
            >
            > Sheet1!D42
            >     ↔ Invoice#9.total
            >
            > PDF page 4 bbox(...)
            >     ↔ Contract#3.effectiveDate
            >
            > image mask(...)
            >     ↔ Building#31
            >
            > video track(...)
            >     ↔ Vehicle#9
            > ```
            >
            > Anchors can point to a whole Thing or only part of one:
            >
            > ```text
            > whole region -> Person#17
            > one text span -> Person#17.name
            > one cell -> Invoice#4.total
            > one mask -> Building#31.roof
            > ```
            >
            > ## 5. Structured sources should mostly be easy
            >
            > For CSV/JSON/XML/XLSX/etc., use their existing structure directly.
            >
            > Support:
            >
            > ```text
            > raw preview
            > exact addressing
            > native values
            > partial projection into NS
            > merge with existing Things
            > transactional mutation where meaningful
            > ```
            >
            > Do not force the entire file into NS if the user only cares about part of it.
            >
            > Example:
            >
            > ```text
            > CSV columns:
            > name
            > email
            > manager
            > salary
            >
            > can map partly to:
            >
            > Person#17.name
            > Person#17.email
            > Person#17.manager -> Ref<Person>
            > Person#17.salary -> Money
            > ```
            >
            > while other columns can remain uninterpreted.
            >
            > If the same Person already exists, prefer anchoring/merging with that existing identity instead of inventing another file-specific Person.
            >
            > ## 6. Text / Markdown / PDF
            >
            > Plain text is just addressable text:
            >
            > ```text
            > TextArtifact
            > └── spans
            > ```
            >
            > Selecting or describing spans can create semantic anchors.
            >
            > Markdown can preserve useful hierarchy:
            >
            > ```text
            > Document
            > ├── heading
            > ├── paragraph
            > ├── list
            > ├── code block
            > └── image/embed
            > ```
            >
            > PDF should preserve both:
            >
            > ```text
            > raw PDF
            > +
            > recoverable native document structure
            > ```
            >
            > including:
            >
            > ```text
            > pages
            > positioned text
            > images
            > tables
            > layout blocks
            > annotations/forms where available
            > ```
            >
            > Pull embedded/secondary media out as native artifacts where useful, but retain provenance back to the parent document.
            >
            > Hygiene matters:
            >
            > ```text
            > original bytes
            > -> decoded structure
            > -> semantic projection
            > ```
            >
            > Keep those layers distinguishable.
            >
            > ## 7. Media: no constant LLM loop
            >
            > This is the important part.
            >
            > For image/video/audio/etc., the model may be needed to establish meaning once:
            >
            > ```text
            > ""this region is Building#31""
            > ""this person is Person#17""
            > ""track this car""
            > ```
            >
            > But **do not call the LLM every frame or every media update**.
            >
            > The desired loop is:
            >
            > ```text
            > initial multimodal interpretation
            >         ↓
            > exact semantic anchor established
            >         ↓
            > LLM chooses/writes minimal conventional feature machinery
            >         ↓
            > cheap deterministic tracking / feature extraction
            >         ↓
            > future artifact diffs
            >         ↓
            > anchor updates
            >         ↓
            > normal Shrine dependency cascade
            > ```
            >
            > The LLM is allowed to use/write Python for this.
            >
            > For now, this can be intentionally ugly:
            >
            > ```text
            > OpenCV
            > segmentation library
            > optical flow
            > keypoints
            > contour extraction
            > histograms
            > embeddings
            > OCR
            > tracking model
            > audio features
            > whatever useful CPU-friendly library exists
            > ```
            >
            > The Python is **not the application** and should not own the semantics.
            >
            > Its purpose is simply:
            >
            > ```text
            > given old artifact state + new artifact state
            > determine how this anchored region/feature changed
            > ```
            >
            > Shrine owns:
            >
            > ```text
            > identity
            > anchor
            > provenance
            > semantic relation
            > dependency
            > history
            > ```
            >
            > ## 8. Cheap features can become native observations
            >
            > Feature extraction does not have to understand meaning.
            >
            > Example:
            >
            > ```text
            > image region
            >     ↓
            > host feature extraction
            >     ├── color histogram
            >     ├── contour
            >     ├── edge density
            >     ├── texture
            >     ├── keypoints
            >     └── motion / mask stats
            > ```
            >
            > Those can be surfaced into NS as ordinary observations:
            >
            > ```text
            > Observation#N
            > ├── subject -> Building#31.roof
            > ├── artifactVersion
            > ├── regionAnchor
            > ├── features
            > └── provenance
            > ```
            >
            > The important distinction:
            >
            > ```text
            > host perception
            >     = what changed physically
            >
            > Foil
            >     = what those changes mean
            > ```
            >
            > ## 9. Let provenance drive Foil crystallization
            >
            > This is the part I care about long-term.
            >
            > Every anchored media change should retain enough provenance that the system can later learn stable semantic relationships.
            >
            > Example:
            >
            > ```text
            > Building#31.roof
            >
            > v1:
            >   mask A
            >   features A
            >
            > v2:
            >   mask B
            >   features B
            >
            > v3:
            >   mask C
            >   features C
            >
            > user correction:
            >   ""that was shadow, not damage""
            > ```
            >
            > Over time the model can inspect:
            >
            > ```text
            > feature histories
            > +
            > anchor changes
            > +
            > native identity
            > +
            > user corrections
            > +
            > downstream outcomes
            > ```
            >
            > and potentially crystallize:
            >
            > ```text
            > RoofFeatures -> DamageState
            > ```
            >
            > into reusable Foil.
            >
            > Then:
            >
            > ```text
            > when roofObservation changes
            >     recompute damageState
            > ```
            >
            > The LLM no longer needs to reinterpret the media every time.
            >
            > So the progression is:
            >
            > ```text
            > FIRST
            > multimodal LLM understands meaning
            >
            > THEN
            > generated conventional feature code maintains observation
            >
            > THEN
            > provenance accumulates
            >
            > THEN
            > model notices stable semantic relation
            >
            > THEN
            > relation crystallizes into Foil
            >
            > EVENTUALLY
            > ordinary native computation handles most future cases
            > ```
            >
            > This is another instance of the broader Shrine rule:
            >
            > **expensive reasoning should collapse into durable software.**
            >
            > ## 10. Bidirectionality
            >
            > Where an anchor supports meaningful writeback:
            >
            > ```text
            > NS mutation
            >     ↓
            > exact anchor
            >     ↓
            > source mutation / derived artifact version
            > ```
            >
            > For structured sources this can often be exact:
            >
            > ```text
            > JSON path
            > XML node
            > XLSX cell
            > CSV field
            > ```
            >
            > For media it depends on the operation:
            >
            > ```text
            > Image.pixels[x,y]
            >     -> exact pixel mutation
            >
            > image mask
            >     -> regional transform
            >
            > video clip/track
            >     -> media edit / derived artifact
            > ```
            >
            > Do not pretend every semantic anchor is writable.
            >
            > Keep read/observe/write capabilities explicit.
            >
            > ## 11. Immediate proving scope
            >
            > Do **not** disappear into universal ingestion architecture.
            >
            > Prove this idea using:
            >
            > ```text
            > image
            > CSV
            > JSON
            > XML
            > text
            > Markdown
            > PDF
            > ```
            >
            > Images are the main semantic-anchor proof.
            >
            > For image:
            >
            > ```text
            > ingest raw file
            > -> retain raw artifact
            > -> native Matrix<Pixel>
            > -> inspect/select region
            > -> correlate selected region with an existing/new NS Thing
            > -> create exact mask/bbox anchor
            > -> mutate image
            > -> use generated/simple feature tracking to maintain correspondence
            > -> emit NS diff
            > -> show dependent presentation updating
            > ```
            >
            > Then modify the image enough that:
            >
            > ```text
            > cheap tracking succeeds
            > ```
            >
            > and later:
            >
            > ```text
            > cheap tracking fails / becomes ambiguous
            > -> escalate to model
            > -> repair
            > -> retain correction/provenance
            > ```
            >
            > For structured files, prove:
            >
            > ```text
            > raw
            > -> native representation
            > -> partial merge into existing NS
            > -> same identity reused elsewhere
            > -> source/native mutation propagates appropriately
            > ```
            >
            > For PDF/MD/text, prove:
            >
            > ```text
            > raw
            > -> native addressable representation
            > -> select exact range/region
            > -> anchor to native Thing/path
            > -> preserve source provenance
            > ```
            >
            > ## 12. Do not overbuild host code
            >
            > Very important:
            >
            > **Do not interpret this as permission to build a giant Python ingestion subsystem.**
            >
            > Most formats are directly legible enough to the model and/or ordinary parsers.
            >
            > Host code should appear mainly where some non-token physical signal has to be maintained over time:
            >
            > ```text
            > media tracking
            > feature extraction
            > OCR when necessary
            > geospatial transforms
            > binary format access
            > sensor processing
            > ```
            >
            > And even then:
            >
            > ```text
            > write the smallest useful adapter
            > expose its outputs natively
            > keep using Shrine
            > ```
            >
            > If the model can inspect something directly, let it.
            >
            > If cheap deterministic computation can maintain the interpretation afterward, compile down to that.
            >
            > ## 13. North star
            >
            > The end state is:
            >
            > ```text
            > foreign artifact
            >     ↓
            > raw source retained
            >     ↓
            > native representation
            >     ↓
            > optional anchored semantic regions
            >     ↓
            > provenance/history
            >     ↓
            > cheap deterministic observation
            >     ↓
            > Foil semantic crystallization
            >     ↓
            > reusable native understanding
            > ```
            >
            > Files and media should stop being opaque blobs, but they also should not force Shrine to mirror every foreign format as ontology.
            >
            > **The raw artifact is evidence. The native representation makes it usable. Anchors connect exact parts of it to the shared world. Provenance lets interpretation improve. Foil is where repeated meaning eventually becomes durable computation.**
            >
            > Build the smallest possible version of this and then immediately dogfood it. Do not stop to generalize it into a framework until actual use forces that generalization."
  "/sha256": "180b9b7582edef4650cc3400188a43bba3851300f8a40878a12b413f3815ea78"
